            "padding_bottom_desktop": "desktop:pb-0",
            "max_width_desktop": "135ch",
            "richtext": "<h4><strong>Why Indoor Air Quality Matters for Kids<\/strong><\/h4><p><\/p><p>Children breathe more air relative to their body size than adults and often spend more time indoors- at home, school, or daycare. That means that what’s in the air can have a greater impact on their health and wellness.  <\/p><p>Common factors that can affect indoor air quality include: <\/p><ul><li>Dust, pollen, and pet dander <\/li><li>Cooking fumes and smoke particles <\/li><li>VOCs (volatile organic compounds), gases released from everyday sources such as cleaning products, paints, and new furniture <\/li><li>Dry or overly humid air that impacts comfort and rest <\/li><li>Seasonal allergens entering from outside <\/li><\/ul><p><\/p><h4><strong>How Poor Indoor Air Quality Can Affect Wellness<\/strong><\/h4><p><\/p><p>Even when the effects aren’t immediately visible , air that’s unbalanced or contains pollutants can impact  how children feel and function: <\/p><ul><li><strong>Sleep disruption- <\/strong>Dry air can irritate the throat and nasal passages, while high  humidity can encourage allergens that interfere with sleep.  <\/li><li><strong>Difficulty focusing- <\/strong> Poor air circulation and exposure to indoor pollutants are linked to reduced attention span and difficullty conentrating, making it harder for children to stay engaged during homework or play.  <\/li><li><strong>Increased sensitivities- <\/strong> Allergens and airborne particles may cause discomfort for children with mild respiratory issues or trigger seasonal reactions. <\/li><li><strong>Lower energy- <\/strong> Exposure to polluted air can reduce oxygen exchange, leaving children more tired and less resilient throughout the day.  <\/li><\/ul>",
            "richtext_alignment": "text-left",
            "richtext_style": "p2"
          }
        },
